Write each position as $bold(x_i) = bold(R) + bold(y_i)$, where $bold(y_i)$ is the position of the $i$-th particle relative to the centre of mass. Then $sum_i m_i bold(y_i) = bold(0)$, and since this holds for all time, $sum_i m_i bold(dot(y)_i) = bold(0)$. The kinetic energy therefore splits as
$
  T = 1/2 M abs(bold(dot(R)))^2 + underbracket(sum_i 1/2 m_i abs(bold(dot(y)_i))^2, "internal kinetic energy"),
$
a centre-of-mass kinetic energy plus an internal kinetic energy.
